  "script": "Biggest NBA legal battles in history! For 35 years, Warner Bros. Discovery had the NBA on TNT, making it one of the longest-running broadcast deals in all of professional sports. So when the NBA's TV rights came up for renewal in 2024, WBD fully expected to keep them, and their executives were publicly calling the negotiations constructive and productive, making investors feel like everything was under control. But that confidence is exactly what ended up getting them into legal trouble. In July 2024, the NBA awarded its new $76 billion media deal to Amazon, Disney, and NBC, completely shutting WBD out. The stock tanked, and the company had to report a $9.1 billion write-down because losing the NBA forced them to reevaluate how much their entire TV network portfolio was actually worth. Shareholders were furious, and one of them, named Richard Collura, filed a class action lawsuit against CEO David Zaslav and CFO Gunnar Wiedenfels, claiming they violated federal securities law by misleading investors about how bad things actually were behind the scenes. But WBD had a defense that basically boiled down to 3 words. They called it fraud by clairvoyance. Their argument was that saying negotiations are constructive doesn't mean you're guaranteeing the outcome, and that media coverage about the risk of losing the NBA was everywhere, meaning investors already had access to that information. In March 2026, a federal judge agreed with them and threw the entire case out.",
